## 🧠 Deep Dive: How Database Locks Are Implemented

Databases implement pessimistic locks via a **lock manager**: an in-memory hash table keyed by (table, row) that tracks which transaction holds each lock and which are waiting. The lock manager periodically scans wait queues for cycles to detect deadlocks, then rolls back one transaction as the victim. Optimistic locking needs no lock manager — the version check happens inside the UPDATE statement itself, handled entirely by the storage engine at commit time.

